# Walking through a proof

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:truestamp_verify,
   github: "truestamp/truestamp-verify-ex", ref: "4c47210adcd20e14e78303e2e406143863c056ae"},
  {:req, "~> 0.7"},
  {:truestamp_verification,
   github: "truestamp/truestamp-verification",
   ref: "aecdfffb01eb27e8ad86e0732f496541091025ab",
   app: false,
   compile: false}
])
```

## What this notebook does

This notebook runs `Truestamp.Verify`, the reference verifier, over one Truestamp proof and
shows what each step of `SPEC.md` section 10.2 found. It prints every value the trace of
section 10.6 holds: for each DERIVE row whose part is a CHECKED value (section 3.7), other
than a row of LINK_HEAD, LINK_KEY_EVENT or LINK_CHAIN_ORDER, the declared value beside the
recomputed one. A few DERIVE rows compare values the trace does not hold; the section on
step 9 names them. Then it runs the same proof online, answering every read from a replay
file, and shows the reads and the submission window they give. A last, optional section
reads the public chains live.

It computes nothing itself: it hashes nothing, verifies no signature, walks no Merkle path
and decodes no file. Every value it prints comes from the verifier, from the report of
`SPEC.md` section 10.5 and the trace of section 10.6, but one: in the section "Change one
declared value", a cell changes the last digit of one declared value in memory and prints
the value it made, and the code cell under "The edited proof, as a verifier meets it" runs
the verifier on the proof with that change. Otherwise the cells only choose a proof, run the
verifier and print what it gives. The library's `test/walkthrough_test.exs` runs every cell
but the first, whose `Mix.install` its own project already satisfies. It fails if a cell
hashes, signs, verifies or decodes anything of its own, or names a module that only the
tests have.

The first cell installs two repositories from GitHub, each pinned to a commit:

* [truestamp-verify-ex](https://github.com/truestamp/truestamp-verify-ex), the library
  `Truestamp.Verify`, with Req for the live section;
* [truestamp-verification](https://github.com/truestamp/truestamp-verification), the
  repository of `SPEC.md`, at the commit the library is tested against. Its conformance
  vectors are the proofs this notebook walks through: the notebook reads those files, and
  the install never compiles them.

The first time, the install also fetches the library's one dependency, the Truestamp Merkle
library, from GitHub, and Req from Hex. To open the notebook, click **Run in Livebook** in
the library's README, which imports this file into your own Livebook, or open
`livebooks/walkthrough.livemd` from a checkout of the library with `livebook server` or the
Livebook desktop app.

## Configuration

This cell picks the proof, and the configuration the verifier runs it under, a
`Truestamp.Verify.Config`. Step 0 of `SPEC.md` section 10.2, CONFIGURATION, fixes E_v, the
verifier's environment (section 6.4), the network pins and the keyring before the file is
read.

The default is the sound item proof `vectors/positive/item.proof.json`. It was signed under
the `test` environment by the key in `vectors/keyring.test.json`, so the run supplies that
keyring (section 8.3), exactly as the conformance case `sound-item` does.

To walk through another proof, change `proof_file`. Any proof under `vectors/positive/`
runs under this configuration: `beacon`, `beacon-empty`, `block`, `block-rotation`,
`block-emergency-rotation`, `entropy_bitcoin`, `entropy_nist`, `entropy_stellar`, `item`,
`item-genesis-witness` and `item-rotation-witness`, each `<name>.proof.json`. To read the
vectors of another checkout of truestamp-verification, set `verification` to its path.
Other configurations:

* keyless mode, where the signature is checked only against the key the proof carries:
  `trust: :keyless`. The label is then `unattributed` unless a row fails (section 8.3);
* a proof from Truestamp's `dev` or `staging` environment: `environment: :dev` or
  `environment: :staging`, with a keyring that holds its key,
  `trust: {:keyring_file, path}`, or keyless mode, `trust: :keyless`. A run whose
  environment is not `prod` needs one of the two (sections 6.4 and 8.3);
* a production proof: `Truestamp.Verify.Config.external!()`, whose environment is `prod`
  and whose keyring is the compiled pin, `spec/keyring.production.json`. That file holds
  no key yet, so every production proof fails KEY_BINDING with `key_binding_failed`
  under it. Until it holds one, walk a production proof with a keyring that holds its key,
  `Truestamp.Verify.Config.external!(trust: {:keyring_file, path})`, or in keyless mode,
  `Truestamp.Verify.Config.external!(trust: :keyless)`;
* a hash to compare with the proof's claims (step 13): add
  `expected_hash: "<lowercase hex>"`.

```elixir
# The checkout of truestamp-verification that the first cell installed.
verification = Path.join(Mix.install_project_dir(), "deps/truestamp_verification")
vectors = Path.join(verification, "vectors")
keyring_file = Path.join(vectors, "keyring.test.json")
proof_file = Path.join(vectors, "positive/item.proof.json")

config =
  Truestamp.Verify.Config.external!(
    environment: :test,
    trust: {:keyring_file, keyring_file}
  )
```

## Printing helpers

These functions only print what the verifier gives. `print_rows` prints report rows as
aligned columns: step, status, reason code (`-` for a pass, which has none) and part, the
JSON Pointer of the member the row is about (`null` for a row about no single member).
`print_trace_row` prints one trace row with its declared value above its recomputed value.
`print_label` prints a report's label, its code, E_v (section 6.4 asks for a non-production
E_v beside every verdict) and, in keyless mode, the words section 8.3 asks for beside every
verdict. `no_derive` says which step ended a run whose trace is empty. Such a run always
has a failed row: a run that passes steps 4 to 7 either stops with a fault
(`internal_error`) at POLICY or DERIVE, or gets through DERIVE, which gives the proof's
`/block/block_hash` a trace row.

```elixir
value_text = fn
  nil -> "null"
  value when is_binary(value) -> value
  value when is_atom(value) -> Atom.to_string(value)
  value -> inspect(value)
end

step_name = &Truestamp.Verify.Step.name/1
label_name = &Truestamp.Verify.Steps.Verdict.label_name/1

print_rows = fn rows ->
  code_width =
    rows |> Enum.map(&String.length(value_text.(&1.code || "-"))) |> Enum.max(fn -> 4 end)

  columns = fn step, status, code, part ->
    IO.puts(
      String.pad_trailing(step, 15) <>
        String.pad_trailing(status, 7) <>
        String.pad_trailing(code, max(code_width, 4) + 2) <> part
    )
  end

  columns.("step", "status", "code", "part")

  Enum.each(rows, fn row ->
    columns.(
      step_name.(row.step),
      value_text.(row.status),
      value_text.(row.code || "-"),
      value_text.(row.part)
    )
  end)
end

rows_of = fn report, steps -> Enum.filter(report.rows, &(step_name.(&1.step) in steps)) end

# Where SPEC.md defines each construction's check, beside the row of section 7.3 it names.
spec_section = %{
  "claims_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (a)",
  "item_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (a)",
  "entropy_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (a)",
  "observation_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (a)",
  "block_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (b)",
  "genesis" => "10.2 step 9 (b)",
  "key_event" => "10.3, key events",
  "handover" => "10.3, key events",
  "epoch_hash" => "10.2 step 9 (c)",
  "bitcoin" => "10.2 step 9 (d)",
  "inclusion" => "10.3, LINK_INCLUSION",
  "witnesses" => "10.3, LINK_WITNESS"
}

print_trace_row = fn row, number ->
  section = Map.get(spec_section, row.construction, "10.2 step 9")

  IO.puts(
    String.pad_leading("#{number}", 3) <>
      "  " <>
      String.pad_trailing(row.construction, 18) <>
      String.pad_trailing(value_text.(row.status), 6) <> "SPEC.md #{section}"
  )

  IO.puts("    part        #{row.part}")
  IO.puts("    declared    #{value_text.(row.declared)}")
  IO.puts("    recomputed  #{value_text.(row.recomputed)}")
  IO.puts("    inputs      #{Enum.join(row.inputs, ", ")}")
end

print_label = fn report ->
  IO.puts("Label        #{label_name.(report.label)}")
  if report.code, do: IO.puts("Code         #{report.code}")
  IO.puts("Environment  #{report.environment}")

  if report.trust_root.kind == :none do
    IO.puts(
      "Keyless      the signature was checked only against the key the document " <>
        "carries (SPEC.md 8.3)"
    )
  end
end

no_derive = fn report ->
  %{step: step, code: code} = Enum.find(report.rows, &(&1.status == :fail))

  "This run ended at #{step_name.(step)} with #{code} (SPEC.md 10.2), before DERIVE " <>
    "compared any declared value, so the trace is empty: there is no DERIVE value to show " <>
    "or change."
end

:ok
```

## Run the verifier

`Truestamp.Verify.verify_file/2` makes steps 0 to 14 over the file. When steps 0 to 3 pass,
it gives `{:ok, report}`, a `Truestamp.Verify.Report`, whatever its label.
`Truestamp.Verify.Report.to_json/1` writes it as the JSON document of section 10.5, which
`mix truestamp.verify` writes to standard output when given the same options (its human
summary goes to standard error). Anything else gives no report, and this cell stops with a
message that says which:

* a failure at steps 0 to 3 is a bare rejection, a `Truestamp.Verify.Rejection`
  (section 10.2, the gate);
* a proof or keyring file that cannot be read is a `Truestamp.Verify.ReadError`, a runtime
  error outside verification (section 12.2, exit code 1), not a step's failure.

The verifier's third error, a `Truestamp.Verify.Fault`, is a fault inside it before steps 0
to 3 passed (sections 10.5 and 12.2). Only a bug in the verifier gives one, so the cell has
no clause for it, and such a bug stops the cell with the fault unmatched.

```elixir
report =
  case Truestamp.Verify.verify_file(proof_file, config) do
    {:ok, report} ->
      report

    {:error, %Truestamp.Verify.ReadError{message: message}} ->
      raise "#{message}. That is a runtime error outside verification, which gives no " <>
              "report (SPEC.md 12.2, exit code 1). Check the paths in the configuration cell."

    {:error, %Truestamp.Verify.Rejection{} = rejection} ->
      why = if rejection.sub_reason, do: " (#{rejection.sub_reason})", else: ""

      raise "The run of #{proof_file} is a bare rejection: #{rejection.code}#{why}. " <>
              "#{rejection.message} A failure at steps 0 to 3 gives no report " <>
              "(SPEC.md 10.2, the gate)."
  end

IO.puts("Proof        #{Path.relative_to(proof_file, verification)}")
IO.puts("Type         #{value_text.(report.type)}")
IO.puts("Subject id   #{value_text.(report.subject_id)}")
IO.puts("Rows         #{length(report.rows)}")
IO.puts("Trace rows   #{length(report.trace)}")
print_label.(report)

:ok
```

## Steps 0 to 6: the gate

`SPEC.md` section 10.2 calls steps 0 to 6 the gate. Nothing in the proof but its
`environment` member is interpreted until the key is bound and the signature verified.

* **0 CONFIGURATION** fixes E_v, the network pins and the keyring that governs
  (sections 6.4, 8.3 and 9).
* **1 READ** and **2 PARSE** apply the parsing rules J1 to J6 (section 10.1).
* **3 ENVELOPE** checks that the parsed document is exactly the envelope of section 3.1,
  however the file is formatted (section 10.1), and that the bytes of the key K pass the
  checks of a key's bytes (section 6.2).
* **4 ENVIRONMENT** compares the proof's `environment` with E_v (section 6.4).
* **5 KEY_BINDING** looks for K's 32 octets in the keyring that governs (sections 6.1 and
  8.3).
* **6 SIGNATURE** verifies the signature V under K over `proof_hash`,
  SHA-256(0x50 || JCS(proof)): the SHA-256 of the byte 0x50 followed by the JCS of the proof
  (sections 6.1 and 6.2). The verifier computed that hash; the notebook only shows its row.

A failure at steps 4 to 6 ends the run: every later step up to EXPECTED_HASH is one row,
`skip prior_failure`, with part null. The attribution says whose key signed the proof, as
far as this run can tell. The trust root says which keys governed key binding: their kind,
the SHA-256 of the keyring's bytes and where the keys came from. In keyless mode those are
`none`, null and null (section 10.5).

```elixir
gate = ~w(CONFIGURATION READ PARSE ENVELOPE ENVIRONMENT KEY_BINDING SIGNATURE)
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, gate))

IO.puts("")
IO.puts("Trust root   #{report.trust_root.kind}")
IO.puts("  digest     #{value_text.(report.trust_root.digest)}")
IO.puts("  location   #{value_text.(report.trust_root.location)}")
IO.puts("Attribution  #{report.attribution}")
```

## Steps 7 and 8: SHAPE and POLICY

**7 SHAPE** (`SPEC.md` section 10.2, step 7) checks `format` and `version`, the JSON Schema
`spec/schema.json`, the checks the schema cannot make, and `constructions`. When it passes,
the report takes the proof's type and subject id. A failure here ends the run as a failure
at the gate does.

**8 POLICY** (step 8) compares every `network` member with the verifier's pins (section 9).
Each comparison is one row.

```elixir
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, ~w(SHAPE POLICY)))
```

## Step 9: DERIVE, and the values its trace holds

DERIVE (`SPEC.md` section 10.2, step 9) recomputes every value the constructions text of
section 7.3 defines from the preimage the proof carries, compares it with the value the
proof declares for it, and checks every link of section 10.3. A proof declares and a
verifier verifies (section 10.6): each hash below was recomputed by the verifier, and the
declared value was only compared with it.

The trace of section 10.6 has one row for each DERIVE row whose part is a CHECKED value,
other than the rows of LINK_HEAD, LINK_KEY_EVENT and LINK_CHAIN_ORDER. Each trace row names
its construction (a row of section 7.3), its part, the declared value, the recomputed value,
the inputs the check read, and its status. This cell prints every value the trace holds.
`Truestamp.Verify.Report.trace_json/1` writes the same rows as a file, and so does
`mix truestamp.verify` with the options that match the configuration cell and
`--trace TRACE`: for the default proof, `mix truestamp.verify
deps/truestamp_verification/vectors/positive/item.proof.json --environment test --keyring
deps/truestamp_verification/vectors/keyring.test.json --trace TRACE`. With `--trace` alone
it runs under E_v `prod`, where every proof under `vectors/positive/` fails
`environment_mismatch` and TRACE holds an empty list. `vectors/positive/<name>.trace.json`
holds these rows for each sound proof.

`recomputed` holds what the check compares `declared` with, as its table defines it
(section 10.6). For example:

* the recomputed hash, for a hash;
* the root the walk reached, for an inclusion proof's root, or null when the walk gives no
  root;
* the record's `leaf_count`, for a `tree_size`;
* 0, for a genesis `height`;
* the `height` and `previous_block_hash` of the block record that carries a rotation or an
  emergency rotation, for the key event's own `height` and `previous_block_hash`;
* the carried `active_key_id`, for the `signing_key_id` of a record that carries a
  prerotation;
* the header's nTime, formatted as a TIME, for a Bitcoin `header_time`;
* for a `handover_signature`, the hex of the 32 octets the signature was verified over.

```elixir
if report.trace == [] do
  IO.puts(no_derive.(report))
else
  report.trace
  |> Enum.with_index(1)
  |> Enum.each(fn {row, number} -> print_trace_row.(row, number) end)
end
```

These DERIVE rows have no trace row:

* LINK_HEAD, LINK_KEY_EVENT and LINK_CHAIN_ORDER (section 10.3). LINK_KEY_EVENT fails with
  `key_event_mismatch` in every run when the key event at `/signing_key_event/block` does
  not introduce K; otherwise, in a run not attributed to Truestamp, it is
  `info not_attributable` (section 10.3, the key-history rows). It is not reported when the
  proof carries no `signing_key_event`.
* For each Bitcoin entry, the two checks of well-formedness of step 9 (d), at
  `raw_transaction` and at `txoutproof`, which compare no values.
* For each Bitcoin entry, the two comparisons of step 9 (d) at the same two parts: the 32
  bytes the transaction's OP_RETURN pushes, against the recomputed `epoch_hash`, and the
  one hash the `txoutproof` matches, against the double SHA-256 of `raw_transaction`.
  Each compares a declared value, the 32 bytes read out of `raw_transaction` or
  `txoutproof`, with a recomputed one. Their parts are PREIMAGE (section 3.7), so they have
  no trace row, and the report gives their declared and recomputed values only when they
  fail (section 10.5); a passing row holds neither. At each of the two parts the
  well-formedness row comes first and the comparison second, in the order of the table of
  step 9 (d).
* The check of a key's bytes at each key event's `public_key` (section 10.3, key events),
  whose part is PREIMAGE too.

Here is every DERIVE row, in the report's order:

```elixir
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, ~w(DERIVE)))
```

## Steps 10 to 12: what an offline run does not establish

**10 CHAIN** and **11 SOURCES** read public chains and entropy sources (section 10.4).
This run has no reader, so it makes no such read and the external gate of section 10.2 is
closed. The section "Reading the chains, from a replay file" below runs the same proof with
one. After a failure at steps 4 to 7, CHAIN and SOURCES are each one row,
`skip prior_failure`, with part null, as every later step is (section 10.2).
Otherwise each CHAIN and SOURCES row is `skip prior_failure` when a row before CHAIN failed,
and `skip offline` when none did. When SHAPE passed and the proof carries
`signing_key_event`, CHAIN ends with KEY_EVENT_COMMITMENT (section 10.3), a row at
`/signing_key_event`, skipped in the same way.

**12 WINDOW** draws the submission window from the reads of this run only (section 11). No
time the proof carries is an edge: not `header_time`, not an entropy payload's time, not
the time inside an id, and not `claims.timestamp`. After a failed row, WINDOW is `skip
prior_failure` (section 11.3, rule 1). Otherwise, with no read, no candidate exists, so
WINDOW gives `info window_not_established` (section 11.3). Either way the report's
`submitted_after` and `submitted_before` are nil, and the run establishes no submission
window: neither the submitted-after edge nor the submitted-before edge. That is not a
failure (section 11.3, "Absence never fails"); it is why an offline run ends
`internally-consistent`, never `verified`, for a sound production proof signed by a key in
the compiled pin (sections 8.3, 10.4 and 10.5). The pin holds no key yet, so for now the
best label an offline run gives a sound production proof is `supplied-keyring`, under a
keyring file that holds its key. A run that makes the reads can draw both edges, but a
block proof, a beacon whose `observations` is empty and an item with no entropy witness
have no submitted-after edge (section 11.2).

When WINDOW is not skipped, its row lists, for each edge that does not exist, the rows that
could have given it a candidate, or that none could.

```elixir
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, ~w(CHAIN SOURCES WINDOW)))

window_row = Enum.find(report.rows, &(&1.step == :window))
absent = window_row.values["absent"] || %{}

IO.puts("")

Enum.each(~w(submitted_after submitted_before)a, fn edge ->
  IO.puts("#{edge}: #{value_text.(Map.fetch!(report, edge))}")

  case Map.get(absent, Atom.to_string(edge)) do
    nil ->
      :ok

    [] ->
      IO.puts("  no row could give it: the proof carries nothing that could (SPEC.md 11.2)")

    reasons ->
      Enum.each(reasons, &IO.puts("  #{&1["code"]} at #{&1["part"]}"))
  end
end)

IO.puts("window: #{report.window}")
```

## Steps 13 and 14: EXPECTED_HASH and VERDICT

**13 EXPECTED_HASH** compares a hash the caller gives with the proof's `claims.hash`
(section 10.2, step 13). Without one, its row is `skip expected_hash_not_checked`, with
part null. After a failure at the gate or at SHAPE, it is `skip prior_failure`, as every
later step is.

**14 VERDICT** gives the label, the attribution and the window, and adds no row (section
10.5). The label is the first of section 10.5's labels that applies, and its meaning below
is in the words of that section's table of labels. E_v is shown beside it (section 6.4).

```elixir
print_rows.(rows_of.(report, ~w(EXPECTED_HASH)))

meaning = %{
  "failed" => "a check failed, and the row says which",
  "unattributed" =>
    "the proof is intact under the key it carries, which was checked against no keyring, " <>
      "so anyone could have made it (section 8.3)",
  "supplied-keyring" =>
    "the proof is intact and signed by a key in the keyring the operator supplied; " <>
      "this says nothing about Truestamp",
  "internally-consistent" =>
    "the proof is intact and signed by Truestamp, but no read in this run established " <>
      "its submitted-before edge",
  "verified" =>
    "the proof is intact, signed by Truestamp, and places its subject's submission " <>
      "before an instant read from a public chain in this run"
}

label = label_name.(report.label)

IO.puts("")
IO.puts("Label        #{label} (SPEC.md 10.5): #{meaning[label]}")

IO.puts("Environment  #{report.environment}")
IO.puts("Attribution  #{report.attribution}")
IO.puts("Verified     #{Truestamp.Verify.verified?(report)}")
IO.puts("Window       #{report.window}")
if report.code, do: IO.puts("Code         #{report.code}")

if report.trust_root.kind == :none do
  IO.puts(
    "Keyless      the signature was checked only against the key the document " <>
      "carries (SPEC.md 8.3)"
  )
end

:ok
```

## Change one declared value

This section changes one value the proof declares, in memory, and runs the verifier on the
result. That run never reaches DERIVE. The signature covers every member of the proof
(section 6.1), so a change to any member fails SIGNATURE, unless it changes `environment`,
which ENVIRONMENT (step 4) checks before SIGNATURE (step 6). The edit here changes a 64-hex
trace value, never `environment`, so its run fails SIGNATURE, and a failure at the gate
ends the run before anything in the proof but `environment` is interpreted (section 10.2,
the gate). A changed declared value can only reach DERIVE in a proof signed again after the
change, and this notebook signs nothing. So the last cell of this section runs the verifier
on such a proof from `vectors/negative/`, which the generator changed and then signed again.

The next cell picks the value to change, and changes its last digit. For an item, a block,
a beacon or an `entropy_nist` proof, it picks by default the part whose value the last
cell's proof changes: `/subject/item_hash`, `/block/block_hash`,
`/observations/entropy_nist/observation_hash` or `/subject/observation_hash`. It does so
when the proof's trace has that row, which a beacon whose `observations` is empty does not.
Otherwise it picks the first trace row whose declared value is 64 lowercase hex digits,
`/block/block_hash` in every sound proof here, and the last cell's proof then changes
another value. Either way, the failing row the last cell prints is the generator's change,
not this cell's edit: the generator took the sound proof
`vectors/positive/<type>.proof.json` for the type the last cell prints (`item`, `block`,
`beacon` or `entropy_nist`), which need not be the proof walked here, changed the first hex
digit of the value, and signed the result again.

To change another value, set `edited_part` to the part of a trace row above whose declared
value is 64 lowercase hex digits, such as `/subject/item_hash` in an item proof or
`/block/block_hash` in any other. The cell changes only such values, and refuses any other
part with a message that says so. It changes the document as the verifier parsed it, which
`Truestamp.Verify.Report.document/1` gives.

```elixir
# nil picks the part the last cell's proof changes, where the trace has it, and otherwise
# the first trace row whose declared value is 64 lowercase hex digits.
edited_part = nil

# For each type that has one, the declared-value vector the last cell runs and the part
# whose value it changes. Any other type, or none, takes the item's vector.
declared_vectors = %{
  "item" => {"declared-item_hash", "/subject/item_hash"},
  "block" => {"declared-block_hash-block", "/block/block_hash"},
  "beacon" =>
    {"declared-observation_hash-observation-nist", "/observations/entropy_nist/observation_hash"},
  "entropy_nist" => {"declared-observation_hash-subject", "/subject/observation_hash"}
}

{vector_name, vector_part} = Map.get(declared_vectors, report.type, declared_vectors["item"])

hex64? = &(is_binary(&1) and &1 =~ ~r/\A[0-9a-f]{64}\z/)
hex_parts = for row <- report.trace, hex64?.(row.declared), do: row.part

edited_part =
  cond do
    report.trace == [] ->
      IO.puts(no_derive.(report))
      nil

    edited_part in hex_parts ->
      edited_part

    edited_part != nil ->
      raise ArgumentError,
            "edited_part must be the part of a trace row above whose declared value is 64 " <>
              "lowercase hex digits, and #{inspect(edited_part)} is not"

    vector_part in hex_parts ->
      vector_part

    true ->
      List.first(hex_parts)
  end

edited_document =
  if edited_part do
    path = ["proof" | edited_part |> String.split("/") |> tl()]
    document = Truestamp.Verify.Report.document(report)
    original_value = get_in(document, path)
    last_digit = if String.ends_with?(original_value, "0"), do: "1", else: "0"
    edited_value = String.slice(original_value, 0..-2//1) <> last_digit

    IO.puts("Changed  #{edited_part}")
    IO.puts("  from   #{original_value}")
    IO.puts("  to     #{edited_value}")

    put_in(document, path, edited_value)
  end

:ok
```

No CHECKED value (section 3.7) sits in an array or under a member name that holds `/` or
`~`, so each segment of a trace part is a member name as it stands, and `get_in` and
`put_in` follow it.

### The edited proof, as a verifier meets it

This cell runs the verifier, under the configuration above, on the edited document.
`Truestamp.Verify.CanonicalForm.encode/1` writes it as a document's bytes; formatting
does not matter to a verifier (section 7.3, the `parsing` row). The steps before SIGNATURE
give the rows they gave above, SIGNATURE fails with `signature_invalid`, and every later
step up to EXPECTED_HASH is one row, `skip prior_failure`, with part null. DERIVE never
reads the edited value.

```elixir
signature_report =
  if edited_document do
    {:ok, signature_report} =
      Truestamp.Verify.verify(Truestamp.Verify.CanonicalForm.encode(edited_document), config)

    signature_report
  end

if signature_report do
  print_rows.(signature_report.rows)
  IO.puts("")
  print_label.(signature_report)
else
  IO.puts(no_derive.(report))
end

:ok
```

### A declared value changed and signed again

Each of the four `declared-*` files this cell can run is a copy of the sound proof of its
type, `vectors/positive/<type>.proof.json` for `item`, `block`, `beacon` or `entropy_nist`,
with one declared value changed, which the generator then signed again with VECTOR_KEY, the
one key of `vectors/keyring.test.json` (sections 9 and 13.2). Each is the proof of a
single-defect case of `SPEC.md` section 13.3, the case whose id is the file's name without
`.proof.json`: such a case changes a sound proof, adjusts every other value so that no
other check fails, and signs the result again with its key. For these four, the Declared
values class of section 13.3 says the changed value fails `derivation_mismatch` at its own
pointer.

This cell runs the one the edit cell picked for the proof's type:
`declared-item_hash` for an item, `declared-block_hash-block` for a block,
`declared-observation_hash-observation-nist` for a beacon and
`declared-observation_hash-subject` for `entropy_nist`. For any other type, and for a run
that failed at steps 4 to 7, whose report gives no type, it takes `declared-item_hash`. It
runs the verifier on the file under the configuration its case in `vectors/cases.json`
gives: environment `test`, the keyring `test` (`vectors/keyring.test.json`), and the network
pins of the `test` row of `spec/network-pins.json`, the default pins under E_v `test`, so
the cell gives no `networks` option.

The file passes the gate, SHAPE and POLICY. DERIVE fails at the changed value, and the
failing trace row gives the declared value beside the recomputed one, which is the value
that sound proof under `vectors/positive/` declares. When the proof walked above is another
one, such as `block-rotation`, that value is not one this notebook showed earlier. A
failure at DERIVE does not end the run. The external gate of section 10.2, closed in an
offline run, then makes each CHAIN and SOURCES row `skip prior_failure` in place of
`skip offline`; a block proof has no SOURCES row. WINDOW gives `skip prior_failure`
(section 11.3, rule 1), EXPECTED_HASH, given no hash, stays
`skip expected_hash_not_checked`, and the label is `failed`.

```elixir
vector_file = Path.join([vectors, "negative", vector_name <> ".proof.json"])

vector_config =
  Truestamp.Verify.Config.external!(
    environment: :test,
    trust: {:keyring_file, keyring_file}
  )

{:ok, vector_report} = Truestamp.Verify.verify_file(vector_file, vector_config)

IO.puts("Proof        #{Path.relative_to(vector_file, verification)}")
IO.puts("Type         #{vector_report.type}")
IO.puts("")
IO.puts("The failing trace rows:")

vector_report.trace
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.filter(fn {row, _number} -> row.status == :fail end)
|> Enum.each(fn {row, number} -> print_trace_row.(row, number) end)

IO.puts("")
IO.puts("ENVIRONMENT to SHAPE, the failing DERIVE rows, and every step after DERIVE:")

print_rows.(
  rows_of.(vector_report, ~w(ENVIRONMENT KEY_BINDING SIGNATURE SHAPE)) ++
    Enum.filter(rows_of.(vector_report, ~w(DERIVE)), &(&1.status == :fail)) ++
    rows_of.(vector_report, ~w(CHAIN SOURCES WINDOW EXPECTED_HASH))
)

IO.puts("")
print_label.(vector_report)

:ok
```

## Reading the chains, from a replay file

An online run gives the verifier a reader, a `Truestamp.Verify.Reader`, and opens the
external gate of section 10.2 when every row before CHAIN passed. CHAIN then confirms each
commitment entry on its pinned network, SOURCES confirms each entropy object at its source,
and each read that confirms an entry gives a candidate for an edge (section 10.4). WINDOW
draws the submitted-before edge from the earliest candidate of the top-level commitment
entries, and the submitted-after edge from the latest candidate of the entropy objects
(section 11).

This section answers every read from a replay file, `Truestamp.Verify.Readers.Replay`: the
answers a reader gave, recorded, so the run reads no network and gives the same rows each
time (section 13.2). It walks the conformance case `window-established`: the sound item
proof, under the configuration of the cells above, with the answers of
`vectors/corpus/replay/window-established.json`. The reads are made now, so each read's
`fetched_at` is the time of this run; the answers are the file's.

For each CHAIN and SOURCES row, the cell prints the reads the row made, each with its kind,
its parameters, the source it was made of and its outcome, `found`, `not_found` or
`unreachable`, and the candidate the row gives. A row's `values` hold these reads by
source, under its source policy (section 10.4, what a row read): this case has one source
for each network, `replay`, and the conformance cases `sources-*` give a network several.
Then it prints each edge: its instant, its basis, the stated assumption the edge rests on
(sections 11.1 and 11.2), its part, and each source it rests on, read in this run or at
the time a cache fetched it (section 11.5).

The label is `supplied-keyring`: the window is established, but the run is attributed to
the keyring this notebook supplied, not to Truestamp. Only a run attributed to Truestamp,
through the compiled pin, the keyring Truestamp publishes or Truestamp's own key chain, can
be `verified` (sections 8.3 and 10.5).

```elixir
replay_file = Path.join(vectors, "corpus/replay/window-established.json")
online_proof_file = Path.join(vectors, "positive/item.proof.json")

online_config =
  Truestamp.Verify.Config.external!(
    environment: :test,
    trust: {:keyring_file, keyring_file},
    reader: Truestamp.Verify.Readers.Replay.new!(replay_file)
  )

{:ok, online_report} = Truestamp.Verify.verify_file(online_proof_file, online_config)

print_reads = fn report ->
  Enum.each(rows_of.(report, ~w(CHAIN SOURCES)), fn row ->
    code = value_text.(row.code || "-")
    IO.puts("#{step_name.(row.step)} #{value_text.(row.part)}: #{row.status} #{code}")

    for outcome <- row.values["outcomes"] || [],
        %{"read" => read, "answer" => answer} <- outcome["reads"] do
      params =
        read
        |> Map.drop(["kind", "network"])
        |> Enum.map_join(fn {name, value} -> " #{name}=#{value}" end)

      IO.puts("  read #{read["kind"]}#{params} from #{outcome["source"]}: #{answer["outcome"]}")
    end

    with %{"instant" => instant, "basis" => basis} <- row.values["candidate"] do
      IO.puts("  candidate #{instant} (#{basis})")
    end
  end)
end

print_edges = fn report ->
  Enum.each(~w(submitted_after submitted_before)a, fn name ->
    case Map.fetch!(report, name) do
      nil ->
        IO.puts("#{name}: null")

      edge ->
        IO.puts("#{name}: #{Truestamp.Verify.Edge.instant_string(edge)}")
        IO.puts("  basis       #{edge.basis}")
        IO.puts("  assumption  #{edge.assumption}")
        IO.puts("  part        #{edge.part}")

        for read <- Truestamp.Verify.Edge.sources(edge) do
          read_when =
            if read.cached,
              do: "at #{DateTime.to_iso8601(read.fetched_at)}",
              else: "in this run"

          IO.puts("  read from   #{read.source} #{read_when}")
        end
    end
  end)

  IO.puts("window: #{report.window}")
end

print_rows.(rows_of.(online_report, ~w(CHAIN SOURCES WINDOW)))
IO.puts("")
print_reads.(online_report)
IO.puts("")
print_edges.(online_report)
IO.puts("")
print_label.(online_report)

:ok
```

## Reading the chains live (optional)

This section reads the public chains themselves: Stellar through Horizon, Bitcoin through
an Esplora server such as mempool.space, and NIST's Randomness Beacon, with the bundled
HTTP sources of `Truestamp.Verify.Online.defaults/0`, under E_v `prod`, with the keyring
Truestamp publishes at `https://www.truestamp.com/.well-known/keyring.json` as the one that
governs (section 8.3). It needs Req, which the first cell installs, and the network.

It runs nothing until `live_proof_file` names a production proof: a proof with no
`environment` member, as Truestamp's production service issues them. The vectors are not
such proofs: they live on test networks no public server holds, and a run under `prod`
refuses each of them at ENVIRONMENT. The rows, the reads and the edges print as in the
section above, but each read names the server it was made to, and a run whose reads find
the proof's entries places its submission in a window drawn from instants this run read.

```elixir
# The path of a production proof to verify online, or nil to skip this section.
live_proof_file = nil

if live_proof_file do
  case Truestamp.Verify.verify_file(live_proof_file, Truestamp.Verify.Online.defaults()) do
    {:ok, live_report} ->
      print_rows.(rows_of.(live_report, ~w(CHAIN SOURCES WINDOW)))
      IO.puts("")
      print_reads.(live_report)
      IO.puts("")
      print_edges.(live_report)
      IO.puts("")
      print_label.(live_report)

    {:error, error} ->
      IO.puts("No report: #{Exception.message(error)}")
  end
else
  IO.puts("Set live_proof_file to the path of a production proof to verify it online.")
end

:ok
```
